      "text": "This problem can be solved using combinations. Since Ace and Zeppo are definitely in the starting lineup, we only need to choose 3 more players from the other 10 players on the team. The number of ways to choose 3 players from 10 can be determined using the combination formula: $\\binom{n}{r} = \\dfrac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}$, where $n$ is the total number of items to choose from and $r$ is the number of items to choose.\n\nHere, $n = 10$ and $r = 3$. So, we have:\n$$\\binom{10}{3} = \\dfrac{10!}{3!(10-3)!} = \\dfrac{10!}{3! \\cdot 7!} = 120$$\n\nTherefore, there are $\\boxed{120}$ different starting lineups possible.\n\nAlternatively, the problem could also be solved using permutations, where order matters. There are 10 players to choose from, and we need to choose 3 players, which can be done in $10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8$ ways, but this includes overcounting because two of the players, Ace and Zeppo, are already chosen. To avoid overcounting, we need to divide by 2 because for each valid starting lineup, there are 2 permutations that would give us the same lineup. Thus, we have:\n$$\\dfrac{10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8}{2} = 120$$\n\nBoth methods give us the same answer - a total of $\\boxed{120}$ different starting lineups possible.",
