      "text": "To solve this problem, we can use the concept of combinations.\n\nFirst, we have 12 players on the team, and we already know that Ace and Zeppo will definitely be in the starting lineup. This means we only need to choose 3 more players from the remaining 10 players.\n\nWe can use the combination formula, which is:\n\n\\[\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\\]\n\nwhere n is the total number of items to choose from, k is the number of items to choose, and ! denotes the factorial function (i.e., the product of all positive integers up to that number).\n\nIn this case, we have n = 10 (the remaining players) and k = 3 (the number of players needed to be chosen).\n\nSo, the number of different starting lineups possible is:\n\n\\[\\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!} = \\frac{10!}{3!7!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\boxed{120}\\]\n\nTherefore, there are 120 different starting lineups possible for Coach Grunt's basketball team, the Grunters.",
